# English versions of the daily concepts (kavramlar.py). Keys: Turkish slug. Same [n] source markers and {id} plant links.
K_EN = {
'animizm': dict(slug='animism', ad='Animism', kisa='Belief in innumerable spiritual beings concerned with human affairs, dwelling in the things of nature.',
 metin=['Animism is the belief in innumerable spiritual beings concerned with human affairs and capable of helping or harming human interests [1]. At its heart is the idea that a tree, a mountain, a river or a plant has a spirit or will of its own.',
        'The English anthropologist Edward Burnett Tylor was the first to survey animistic beliefs systematically, in his 1871 work “Primitive Culture” [1]. The term is still used for that reason, although Tylor’s view of religions as ranked from “primitive” to “advanced” was later criticised by anthropologists.',
        'Many traditional relationships with plants rest on an animist worldview. Even the name of the Amazonian {ayahuasca} vine says so: it comes from the Quechua words “aya” (spirit) and “waska” (vine), the “vine of the spirits”.']),
'kam': dict(slug='kam', ad='Kam', kisa='The Altai and Tatar Turkic word for the shaman: an intermediary who travels to the sky and the underworld.',
 metin=['In Altai and Tatar Turkic the shaman is called “kam” (gam). The word appears in Old Uyghur texts, in the Kutadgu Bilig and in the Codex Cumanicus [1].',
        'In the kam’s world the cosmos is divided into three layers: sky, earth and underworld. Among the Altai, the benevolent being of the sky is Ülgen and the ruler of the underworld is Erlik Khan [1].',
        'The kam’s most important tool is the drum, called “tüngür” or “çaluu”. Its frame is made of birch or cedar chosen from untouched forest, and its face is painted with the sun, moon, animals and trees [1]. Purifying a place with {ardic} smoke before ceremonies is also a known practice of Central Asian shamanism.']),
'nazar': dict(slug='evil-eye', ad='Nazar (the evil eye)', kisa='The belief that an envious or admiring gaze can cause harm; in Anatolia its best-known protector is Syrian rue.',
 metin=['Nazar, or the evil eye, is when someone who looks at something with admiration, longing or envy affects it through their gaze in a way that causes harm [1].',
        'Using plants against the evil eye is very common in Anatolia. First among them is {uzerlik}: its dried capsules are strung and hung at the door, and its seeds are burned on embers as incense at weddings, births and moves to a new home.',
        'The same belief appears elsewhere: in Peru and Bolivia {sedef} is rubbed on the body against “mal de ojo”.']),
'pachamama': dict(slug='pachamama', ad='Pachamama', kisa='Mother Earth of the Andes, “fed” with offerings in August.',
 metin=['Pachamama is the earth-mother figure of the Andean peoples. Reverence for her is especially widespread on the Altiplano of Peru and Bolivia and is a tradition distinct from the Inca sun religion [1].',
        'Pachamama is believed to need to “eat and drink”, so an offering called a “despacho” or “payment” is prepared for her. As described by a ritual master’s family from Peru, it includes {koka} leaves, corn, seeds, sweets, white, pink and red carnation petals, a seashell and incense, while chicha or another drink is sprinkled on the earth. The family makes the offering in August [2].',
        'Its meaning is reciprocity: the family shares its food and drink with the earth, and the earth feeds and protects them [2].']),
'limpia': dict(slug='limpia', ad='Limpia', kisa='A cleansing ritual of Latin American folk healing, using plants, an egg and smoke.',
 metin=['“Limpia” means cleansing in Spanish; it is a spiritual cleansing ritual within Latin American folk healing (curanderismo), usually performed by a curandera or curandero [1].',
        'An egg is passed slowly around the body; plants such as rosemary, rue and basil are brushed over it; copal or incense is burned [1]. People seek a limpia after grief, stress, illness, heartbreak, a move or bad luck [1].',
        'In the markets of Peru and Bolivia {sedef} (rue) is the key plant of the limpia, rubbed on the body against the evil eye. {biberiye}, {isirgan} and {molle} are also used in Andean limpias.']),
'ogretmen-bitkiler': dict(slug='teacher-plants', ad='Teacher plants', kisa='Plants “with a mother” that Peruvian Amazonian healers believe give knowledge to their apprentices.',
 metin=['Healers in East-Central Peru call a group of plants at the heart of initiation and apprenticeship “plantas con madre”, plants with a mother. They are believed to guide the apprentice towards sacred knowledge, plant use and healing [1].',
        'The plants are taken within a strict regime called the “dieta”, supervised by an experienced master healer (maestro curandero). A 2011 field study with Mestizo and Shipibo-Konibo healers recorded 55 species used in this way [1]. The dieta is a retreat-like practice combining restrictions on social life, behaviour and diet with specially prepared plants [2].',
        '{chiricsanango} is known as one of these teacher plants; its name means “the one that makes you cold” in Quechua. Many of these plants are potent and are used within their own tradition under a master’s guidance; they appear here as a cultural record.']),
'haoma-ve-soma': dict(slug='haoma-and-soma', ad='Haoma and soma', kisa='The sacred plant and drink of Zoroastrianism and the soma of the Vedas: a plant whose identity is still debated.',
 metin=['In Zoroastrianism, haoma is the name of both a sacred plant and the drink made from it. Pounding the plant to prepare the drink, and drinking it, are central to Zoroastrian ritual. Haoma is also personified as a deity who grants health, fertility and even immortality [1].',
        'Haoma is the Avestan form of the Sanskrit “soma”. The close similarity of the two rituals points scholars to a shared Indo-Iranian religion older than Zoroastrianism [1].',
        'Which plant haoma was is unknown. One candidate is {efedra}: it has been debated whether this plant, which grows in Asia and has been used in Chinese medicine for at least 2,000 years, could be haoma and soma.']),
'benzerlik-ogretisi': dict(slug='doctrine-of-signatures', ad='Doctrine of signatures', kisa='The old belief that a plant’s shape or colour shows which ailment it treats.',
 metin=['The doctrine of signatures is the belief that a plant’s shape, colour or appearance is a sign of which organ or illness it can help. Its roots go back to Pliny and Dioscorides; it was developed above all by the Swiss physician and alchemist Paracelsus and became widespread in the West especially in the 16th and 17th centuries [1]. In his “Phytognomonica” of 1588, Giambattista della Porta pictured eyebright, which resembles an eye, and maidenhair fern against hair loss [1].',
        'Examples are as many as the plants: bloodroot with its scarlet root was thought to treat the circulation, yellow dandelion jaundice and the liver, and hepatica, whose lobes resemble the liver, liver ailments [2]. The root of {adamotu} was likened to male genitalia and so held to cure infertility [2]. Mandrake is poisonous; it appears here only as a cultural record.',
        'According to the biologist Bradley Bennett there is no evidence that a plant’s form ever led to the discovery of its medicinal properties; “signatures” are explanations added afterwards, not predictions made beforehand [3]. Bennett showed that of 80 species chosen at random from plants with heart-shaped leaves, only 3 were used for the heart [1]. Still, once taste and smell are included the doctrine has some truth: strongly scented or bitter plants appear often in old pharmacopoeias. In Bennett’s view, the doctrine worked best as a memory aid that made it easier to pass knowledge down the generations [3].']),
'tibb-i-nebevi': dict(slug='prophetic-medicine', ad='Prophetic medicine (tıbb-ı nebevî)', kisa='“The Prophet’s medicine”: the Prophet Muhammad’s sayings on health and healing, and the books that grew from them.',
 metin=['Tıbb-ı nebevî means “the Prophet’s medicine” and refers to two things at once: the hadiths of the Prophet Muhammad on illness, treatment and the preservation of health, and the literature formed by collecting these hadiths. It is treated as part of the wider tradition of Islamic medicine [1].',
        'The first work to present medicine through hadiths is said to be “al-Tibb al-nabawi” by Ibn Habib al-Sulami (d. 853). The most famous book of the genre is the work of the same name by Ibn Qayyim al-Jawziyya (d. 1350), which is in fact the medical section of his “Zad al-ma‘ad”. Abu Nu‘aym al-Isfahani, al-Dhahabi and al-Suyuti also wrote in this field; in the Ottoman period the “Tıbb-ı Nebevî” of Hüseyin Remzi (d. 1896) stands out [1].',
        'According to the TDV Encyclopedia of Islam, the hadiths in this literature are judged by the same criteria as those in other hadith books: alongside authentic reports there are weak and fabricated ones, and some texts reflect the medical understanding of their time [1].',
        'The recommendation of {corekotu} for certain illnesses is among the best-known examples in this field [1]. The popularity of black seed oil as a household remedy in Anatolia, remembered as a “cure for everything but death”, comes from this hadith tradition. How far the plant is supported by science today is a separate question, answered on the black seed page.']),
'kina-gecesi': dict(slug='henna-night', ad='Henna night (kına gecesi)', kisa='The night, a few days before a wedding, when henna is applied to the bride’s hands; the best-known henna custom in Turkish culture.',
 metin=['The kına gecesi, or henna night, is held a few days before a wedding, when henna is applied to the bride’s hands; it is the best-known of the henna customs in Turkish culture [1]. {kina} is the name both of the plant Lawsonia inermis and of the powder or paste made from its leaves; the word came into Turkish from the Arabic “hinna” [1].',
        'Henna is not only for brides. It is applied to the palm of a boy at his circumcision, to young men being sent off to military service, on religious holidays and when welcoming pilgrims home. The foreheads, tails and legs of horses were dyed with it, and rams given as gifts were marked with henna. Henna-night feasts were also held at the circumcision ceremonies of Ottoman princes [1].',
        'The custom has old roots. In ancient Egypt henna was used to dye hair and cloth and in mummification [1]. In hadiths reported from Aisha, henna appears as a sign that distinguishes a woman’s hand from a man’s; scholars took this as a recommendation for women to use henna [1].',
        'The hand dyed on the henna night marks a passage from one life to another: a girl becomes a bride, a young man becomes a soldier. Henna therefore stands for both devotion and farewell. In North Africa and South Asia, the “mehndi” patterns drawn on hands and feet at weddings are the same plant used at the same kind of passage.']),
'ayurveda': dict(slug='ayurveda', ad='Ayurveda', kisa='India’s traditional system of medicine: three bodily humours, five elements and a great many plants.',
 metin=['Ayurveda is the traditional system of Indian medicine; it aims both to prevent and to cure illness and is still widely practised in parts of Asia [1].',
        'Ayurveda’s earliest concepts appear in the Atharvaveda, dated to about the 2nd millennium BCE. Of its core texts, the Caraka-samhita is attributed to the physician Caraka and dates in its present form to the 1st century CE; the Susruta-samhita, attributed to the surgeon Susruta, probably originated in the last centuries BCE and took its present form by the 7th century CE [1].',
        'Ayurveda explains the body in terms of earth, water, fire, air and ether and of three bodily humours called vata, pitta and kapha. The herbs recommended for treatment are numerous, and herbal medicines are part of both cure and prevention [1]. The Indian Medical Council, set up by the government of India in 1971, recognises Ayurveda together with Unani and Siddha; Ayurvedic practitioners work mostly in rural areas and serve at least 500 million people in India alone [1].',
        '{ashwagandha} is one of Ayurveda’s best-known plants: it is considered a “rasayana”, or rejuvenator, that gives strength and stamina, and the powdered root is boiled in milk and drunk. Its rise in recent years to one of the world’s best-selling supplements for stress and sleep shows how a plant from a traditional system can travel to the global market.']),
'yerli-amerikan-kilisesi': dict(slug='native-american-church', ad='Native American Church', kisa='A North American religious movement that holds peyote sacred and combines Indigenous and Christian beliefs.',
 metin=['The Native American Church is the most widespread Indigenous religious movement among North American Indians and is considered one of the most influential forms of Pan-Indianism. It is also called the Peyote Religion or Peyotism [1].',
        '{peyote} was used for visions and healing in pre-Columbian Mexico; from the mid-19th century its use spread north into the Great Plains. It probably became a distinct religion around 1885 among the Kiowa and Comanche in Oklahoma and reached Canada after 1891. The church was incorporated in 1918 [1].',
        'Its beliefs combine Indigenous and Christian elements in varying degrees: there is one supreme God (the Great Spirit) who works through spirits, while peyote is personified as the Peyote Spirit and eaten as a sacrament to commune with God and the spirits. The ceremony usually begins on Saturday evening in a tepee around a crescent-shaped earthen altar and a sacred fire; it continues all night with prayer, singing and water rites and ends on Sunday morning with a shared breakfast. The way of life known as the “Peyote Road” teaches brotherly love, care of family, steady work and avoidance of alcohol [1].',
        'Peyote was banned by government agents in 1888 and later by 15 states; in the 1960s religious-freedom appeals were upheld by several state supreme courts [1]. Peyote is a potent and legally restricted plant; it appears here as a cultural record.']),
'geleneksel-cin-tibbi': dict(slug='traditional-chinese-medicine', ad='Traditional Chinese medicine', kisa='A system of medicine at least 23 centuries old that seeks to prevent illness by keeping yin and yang in balance.',
 metin=['Traditional Chinese medicine is a system at least 23 centuries old that seeks to prevent or treat illness by maintaining the balance between yin and yang. Its earliest known written source is the “Huangdi neijing” (The Yellow Emperor’s Inner Classic), from the 3rd century BCE [1].',
        'In this system the body’s life force, qi, flows through channels called meridians. The five phases (wuxing) of wood, fire, earth, metal and water are used to identify imbalances. Treatments include brews made from thousands of plants, acupuncture, cupping and moxibustion, in which cones usually made from an Artemisia, the same genus as {pelin}, are burned at acupuncture points. Li Shizhen’s “Bencao gangmu”, published in 1578, lists 1,892 drugs and about 11,000 prescriptions [1].',
        'Some herbal formulas have been in use for more than 2,200 years. Nearly 200 modern medicines have come from about 7,300 medicinal plant species used in China; ephedrine, used for asthma, was first isolated from mahuang, that is {efedra} [2].',
        '{ginseng} is almost the emblem of this tradition. The root of Asian ginseng has long been used as a drug in China, Korea and Japan and is made into a stimulating tea. The Chinese regarded it as a panacea, but used it more to prevent illness than to cure it [3].']),
'mesa': dict(slug='mesa', ad='Mesa', kisa='The ritual altar of northern Peruvian folk healing, around which San Pedro ceremonies are held.',
 metin=['At the heart of folk healing (curanderismo) in the coastal and highland regions of northern Peru are ceremonies held around a ritual altar called the “mesa”. They often take place near sacred hills or lagoons and aim mainly to remove the negative forces that cause illness [1].',
        'The plant of the ceremony is the {sanpedro} cactus. It is used as a guide to seeing and diagnosing illness. The patient is prepared over weeks or even months, and the brew made from the cactus is prepared by the master healer, the “maestro curandero”, or an assistant [1].',
        'By a decision announced in the official gazette El Peruano on 17 November 2022, Peru’s Ministry of Culture declared the traditional knowledge, practices and uses of the San Pedro cactus part of the Cultural Heritage of the Nation. The application came from La Libertad and was prepared with the culture offices of Tumbes, Piura, Lambayeque, Cajamarca, Amazonas and Áncash. The decision states that the tradition has pre-Hispanic roots, later joined by Christian elements, and reflects a holistic worldview [1].',
        'The plant’s name shows this blending too: the Spanish named the cactus after Saint Peter, who holds the keys of heaven. San Pedro is a potent plant used within its own tradition under a master’s guidance; it appears here as a cultural record.']),
'smudging': dict(slug='smudging', ad='Smudging', kisa='Burning bundles of dried plants to cleanse with their smoke; a practice that passed from North American Indigenous ceremonies to the New Age movement.',
 metin=['Smudging is the burning of bundles of dried plants for ritual purposes, using their smoke. Cleansing with smoke has a long history in religious ceremonies in many parts of the world [1].',
        'North American Indigenous peoples use these bundles (smudge sticks) in traditional ceremonies; they have burned them to purify homes after illness and for success in hunting [1]. {akadacayi}, the sacred plant of the Kumeyaay, Chumash and Cahuilla of southern California, is also burned in bundles in cleansing ceremonies. More recently the New Age movement adopted the bundles and began using them to clear spaces of unwanted energies [1].',
        'In 2024 researchers from Utrecht University examined 29 bundles sold in shops and online in the Netherlands: ten contained or were made of white sage, and some contained North American Artemisia species. Rising global demand may lead to unsustainable wild harvesting of white sage, harming both the wild plant and the Indigenous communities that rely on it for their ceremonies [1].',
        'According to the researchers, the Netherlands has its own traditions of burning blessed plants, so there is no need to bring plants from the other side of the world; they suggest {lavanta}, {biberiye}, tansy and rose instead [1]. Cleansing with {ardic} smoke in Anatolia and with arça in Central Asia belongs to the same broad family of traditions.']),
'ikaro': dict(slug='icaro', ad='Icaro', kisa='The healing songs that healers in the Peruvian Amazon believe they learn from plant spirits.',
 metin=['Healers who work with plants in the Peruvian Amazon believe that much of their knowledge comes through songs. These melodies, called “icaros”, are taught to them by the spirits of plants in visions or dreams during the dieta. According to the anthropologist Luis Eduardo Luna, who worked with four mestizo healers around Iquitos in the early 1980s, the number and strength of a healer’s icaros are the best measure of his knowledge and power [1].',
        'Icaros have many functions: strengthening or weakening visions, calling protective spirits, curing specific illnesses, reinforcing a medicinal plant, treating snakebite, awakening love, calling rain or wind. Some protect the healer from rival sorcerers. One of the healers interviewed knew 60–70 icaros; the others said they knew over a hundred [1].',
        'Icaros are sung mostly in Quechua, sometimes in Indigenous languages such as Cocama or Omagua, or a mixture. The healers say they learned these languages not from Indigenous people but from the plant spirits; singing in a native language carries power and prestige [1]. Icaros are often sung while blowing the smoke of {mapacho} tobacco.',
        'Shipibo-Konibo healers also say that the mother of tobacco taught them the icaros and how to blow smoke over the patient [2]. This is a cultural record, not a recommendation for ritual use.']),
'vegetalismo': dict(slug='vegetalismo', ad='Vegetalismo', kisa='The tradition of mestizo healers in the Peruvian Amazon who are believed to learn from plants.',
 metin=['Mestizo healers who work with plants around Iquitos are called “vegetalistas”, plant specialists. The name sets them apart from “oracionistas”, who work only with prayer, and “espiritistas”, who work only with spirits. Among vegetalistas there are specialisms: the “purguero” who uses ayahuasca, the “tabaquero” who works mainly with tobacco, the “camalonguero” who uses camalonga seeds, the “perfumero” who works with flower essences, and others [1].',
        'Asked where their knowledge came from, all four healers Luna interviewed gave the same answer: “La purga misma te enseña”, “the purge [ayahuasca] itself teaches you”. For them many plants, perhaps all, have a “mother” or spirit; these spirits teach diagnosis, which plant to use and how, the use of tobacco smoke, and the icaros [1].',
        'The “teacher plants” include {mapacho} tobacco, {toe}, {chuchuhuasi}, {abuta}, {chiricsanango}, bobinsana, ajo sacha and many forest trees. Some are added to ayahuasca; others are taken alone. According to Luna, what they share is that they cause visions, dizziness, vomiting or very vivid dreams [1]. A healer Luna worked with calls tobacco “the king of all plants”; most healers in the region work with tobacco alone. In his view the master’s task is not to teach medicine, which the plant does, but to protect the apprentice and show how the plants are prepared and taken [2].',
        'The way to learn is a strict diet: no salt, sugar, fat, alcohol, pork, chicken, fruit or cold drinks; the staple is cooked plantain and smoked fish. The food is prepared by the apprentice himself or by a girl who has not yet begun to menstruate or a woman past menopause; the diet can last months or even years [1]. This is a cultural record; many of these plants are poisonous or potent.']),
'susto': dict(slug='susto-mal-aire', ad='Susto and mal aire', kisa='In Andean and Amazonian folk medicine: the soul fleeing the body after a fright, the blow of bad air, and illness caused by sorcery.',
 metin=['The complaints most often brought to folk healers in northern Peru are illnesses such as “mal aire” or “mal viento” (bad air, bad wind), “susto” or “espanto” (fright), “mal ojo” (evil eye) and “daño” (harm caused by sorcery). Their causes are described as sudden changes in temperature, a great fright, a spell cast by someone else, or poisoned food [1]. Of the 510 medicinal plant species recorded in the region, 207, or 40 per cent, are used for such “magical” illnesses [2].',
        'In southern Peru susto is described as the soul (ánimo) leaving the body after a great fright; children are thought to be most affected. It is treated with a night-time soul-calling ceremony: a mesa is set up, candles and incense are lit and the patient’s belongings are laid out [3].',
        'In the Amazon, healers describe “mal aire” as the blow of a bad spirit, causing pallor, shivering, vomiting, diarrhoea and fever. “Cutipa” is harm from touching a powerful being such as a sloth or a river dolphin, or from breaking food taboos. Healers tell these apart by feeling the pulse and the hands and feet, and treat them with {mapacho} tobacco, icaros, emetic plants, baths and diet [4].',
        'Treatment ranges from a simple cleansing (limpia) with sprayed perfume and holy water in mild cases to an all-night mesa ceremony with the {sanpedro} cactus in serious ones. Afterwards the patient is given a “flowering” bath and may receive a protective flask (seguro) of herbs, perfume, saints’ images and the patient’s own hair and nails [2]. Similar beliefs are known in Anatolia as fright, the evil eye and being “struck by a jinn”, for which people turn to ocak healers and lead pouring.']),
'dieta': dict(slug='dieta', ad='Dieta (sama)', kisa='The regimen in the Peruvian Amazon in which a plant remedy is taken without salt, nearly fasting and in seclusion.',
 metin=['In the Peruvian Amazon, healing with plants is not thought to come from the plant alone. As the San Martín Quechua of the Chazuta valley put it, “en la dieta está la curación”: the healing is in the diet itself. While taking a plant remedy people rest and avoid heavy food, alcohol, perfume and strong smells, and limit sex. If these rules are broken the diet is believed to be “cut” (cortar la dieta) or to “cross” with the plant and cause harm (cruzar la dieta) [1].',
        'The strictest form is the diet called “sama” in Quechua: the plant remedy is taken while nearly fasting and away from people for two to eight weeks. The researchers interviewed 140 people and recorded 122 strict diets and 106 plant species used in them; among the most frequent are Tovomita species, bobinsana, {chiricsanango} and {ajosacha} [1].',
        'In Chazuta a young man’s first strict diet marks his passage to adulthood. Men dieted to grow strong as farmers, hunters and workers, and in the past before and after military service. Hunters hope to lose their body odour so animals do not notice them; even hunting dogs are put on diets. The vivid dreams, songs heard and snakes met during a diet are taken as the plant spirits testing the dieter [1].',
        'The diet is ended by “cutting” it with salt; breaking the rules, above all through sex, is considered the most dangerous breach and is treated by blowing tobacco smoke [2]. This is a cultural record; long fasting and many of these plants are dangerous without medical supervision.']),
'cadi-merhemi': dict(slug='witches-ointment', ad='Witches’ ointment', kisa='The ointment of nightshade plants that witches in medieval Europe were believed to rub on to “fly”.',
 metin=['Since antiquity, the intoxicating drinks of almost every culture have included plants of the nightshade family: mandrake, {banotu}, {tatula} and deadly nightshade. Their active compounds are atropine and scopolamine; at moderate doses they cause hallucinations, at high doses they stop breathing [1].',
        'In medieval Europe these plants became the basis of the green ointment known as “witches’ ointment”. According to the accounts, a witch would undress, rub the ointment over her whole body, sit astride a broom or a stove poker, and the night journey would begin. People numbed by the ointment dreamed of flying, banquets, dancing and love, and on waking believed that what they had dreamed was real. One researcher saw the ointment as a means of intoxication for the poor, who could not afford costlier pleasures [1].',
        'The same plants were used in love potions, poisons and sleep sponges. Of the 140 remedies in the famous twelfth-century formulary Antidotarium Nicolai, at least 29 contained opium, 19 henbane and 14 mandrake. Love potions were banned under heavy penalties from the 13th century; in Prussian law the ban lasted into the 18th century [1].',
        'In the nineteenth century chemists isolated the active compounds of these plants; hyoscine from henbane was for a time used to sedate psychiatric patients [1]. All these plants are highly poisonous; they appear here as a cultural and historical record.']),
}
